{"meta":{"chapter":"01","section":"01","title":"Introduction to Dynamics","chapter_title":"Foundational Kinematics","course":"dynamics","description":"Differentiate kinematics from kinetics and interpret velocity and acceleration in engineering motion problems.","estimated_time_minutes":12,"learning_objectives":["Understand the difference between statics and dynamics","Define position, velocity, and acceleration","Interpret the signs of velocity and acceleration in one-dimensional motion","Explain why engineers separate describing motion from explaining its causes"],"concept_ids":["kinematics","dynamics","velocity","acceleration"],"prereq_section_ids":[],"practice_ids":["rectilinear_concept_1","rectilinear_concept_2","rectilinear_concept_3","rectilinear_concept_4","rectilinear_numeric_1","rectilinear_numeric_2","rectilinear_numeric_3","rectilinear_numeric_4","rectilinear_step_1","rectilinear_step_2"]},"content":"# Introduction to Dynamics\n\n> **\"Give me matter and motion, and I will construct the universe.\"**  \n> - attributed to Rene Descartes\n\n---\n\n## Opening: A Landing That Lives or Dies on Acceleration\n\nOn August 5, 2012, the Mars rover Curiosity hit the top of the Martian atmosphere at about 5,900 m/s and had roughly seven minutes to reach a dead stop on the surface. No human could intervene; the radio delay to Earth was about 14 minutes, so the entire descent was over before mission control even saw it begin. Engineers at JPL called it \"seven minutes of terror.\" Every parachute deployment, every retro-rocket burn, and the final sky-crane lowering had been timed by solving one family of problems: given the forces acting on the spacecraft, how does its velocity change, and where will it be at each instant?\n\nThat is the entire job of dynamics. **Dynamics studies how motion changes when forces act on a body.** In statics, we were satisfied once the net force and net moment were zero. In dynamics, that same imbalance is the whole story: if a body's velocity changes in magnitude or direction, something in the force environment is causing it.\n\nThat shift matters because engineers rarely design for perfectly frozen systems. Cars brake, drones pitch forward, robotic arms accelerate into place, and packaging lines speed up and slow down to protect products. Dynamics gives us the language to describe those changes precisely enough to predict them, and prediction (not description) is what lets a team land a one-ton rover on another planet without a second chance.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Statics asks whether a structure will hold still. Dynamics asks where a body will be, how fast it will be going, and what force it will take to get it there. Everything downstream (braking distance, actuator sizing, impact loads, ride comfort) is built on that question.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Distinguish** kinematics from kinetics and state which quantities belong to each.\n2. **Interpret** the signs of velocity and acceleration to describe one-dimensional motion correctly.\n3. **Relate** position, velocity, and acceleration through differentiation, $v = dx/dt$ and $a = dv/dt$.\n4. **Apply** Newton's second law, $\\sum \\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$, to compute the acceleration produced by a known force.\n5. **Identify** the modeling assumptions (particle, constant mass, inertial frame) behind a first dynamics model and judge when they break down.\n\n---\n\n## The First Big Distinction: Kinematics vs. Kinetics\n\nDynamics is usually split into two complementary viewpoints:\n\n- **Kinematics** describes motion itself: position, displacement, velocity, and acceleration.\n- **Kinetics** explains why that motion happens by connecting it to force, mass, and moment.\n\nYou can think of kinematics as the \"what is the body doing?\" layer, and kinetics as the \"what made it do that?\" layer. Good engineers move back and forth between both.\n\n| Viewpoint | Question it answers | Core quantities | Governing relation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Kinematics | What is the motion? | $x$, $v$, $a$, $t$ | $v = \\dfrac{dx}{dt}$, $a = \\dfrac{dv}{dt}$ |\n| Kinetics | Why does it move that way? | $F$, $m$, $a$ | $\\sum \\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$ |\n\nThe link between the two columns is **acceleration**. Kinematics treats $a$ as something to be measured or integrated; kinetics treats $a$ as something forces *cause*. The same symbol bridges geometry and force, which is why mastering its meaning is the single most important habit in this course.\n\n<kinematics-diagram></kinematics-diagram>\n\n## Motion Is More Than Speed\n\nStudents often treat motion as if it only means \"how fast something is going.\" Dynamics is richer than that. A body can:\n\n- move fast with **zero acceleration** if its velocity stays constant\n- move slowly with **large acceleration** if its velocity is changing rapidly\n- have positive velocity and negative acceleration when it is moving in the positive direction but slowing down\n\nThis is where sign conventions become important. In one-dimensional motion, the sign of velocity tells you direction. The sign of acceleration tells you how velocity is changing. Those are not the same question.\n\n### A Quick Engineering Reading of the Signs\n\nSuppose we define motion to the right as positive.\n\n| Case | Velocity | Acceleration | Physical meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | $v > 0$ | $a > 0$ | Moving right and speeding up |\n| 2 | $v > 0$ | $a < 0$ | Moving right and slowing down |\n| 3 | $v < 0$ | $a < 0$ | Moving left and speeding up |\n| 4 | $v < 0$ | $a > 0$ | Moving left and slowing down |\n\nThe decisive rule: a body **speeds up when $v$ and $a$ share a sign** and **slows down when they have opposite signs**. That table is the beginning of nearly every rectilinear motion model in this course.\n\n---\n\n## The Calculus Bridge: From Position to Acceleration\n\nThe three quantities are tied together by differentiation. If position is $x(t)$, then\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\frac{dx}{dt}, \\qquad a(t) = \\frac{dv}{dt} = \\frac{d^2x}{dt^2}.\n$$\n\nReading the chain in reverse, velocity is the time-integral of acceleration and position is the time-integral of velocity:\n\n$$\nv(t) = v_0 + \\int_0^t a\\,dt', \\qquad x(t) = x_0 + \\int_0^t v\\,dt'.\n$$\n\nWhen acceleration is **constant**, these integrals collapse into the familiar constant-acceleration equations. Integrating $a = \\text{const}$ once gives $v = v_0 + at$. Integrating again gives\n\n$$\n\\boxed{x = x_0 + v_0 t + \\tfrac{1}{2} a t^2}.\n$$\n\nEliminating $t$ between the two yields the time-free relation\n\n$$\n\\boxed{v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\,(x - x_0)}.\n$$\n\nThese follow only when $a$ is constant. The moment acceleration varies with time, position, or velocity, you must return to the integral forms. Forgetting that restriction is one of the most common errors in introductory dynamics.\n\nThe explorer below lets you drive the constant-acceleration model directly. Set an initial speed and a (signed) acceleration, then read off the position, the current velocity, and the stopping distance at the chosen time. Watch how the stopping distance responds when you change the initial speed: that quadratic sensitivity is the whole reason highway speed limits exist.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Constant-acceleration motion (x = x0 + v0 t + 1/2 a t^2)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"v0\",\"label\":\"Initial speed v0\",\"min\":0,\"max\":40,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"default\":12},{\"name\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Acceleration a\",\"min\":-10,\"max\":10,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"m/s^2\",\"default\":-3},{\"name\":\"t\",\"label\":\"Elapsed time t\",\"min\":0,\"max\":10,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"s\",\"default\":4}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Velocity v = v0 + a t\",\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"expr\":\"v0 + a * t\"},{\"label\":\"Position x = v0 t + 1/2 a t^2\",\"unit\":\"m\",\"expr\":\"v0 * t + 0.5 * a * t^2\"},{\"label\":\"Stopping distance v0^2 / (2|a|)\",\"unit\":\"m\",\"expr\":\"v0 * v0 / (2 * sqrt(a * a))\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"v0\",\"output\":2}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check\n  question=\"In the explorer, you double the initial speed v0 while keeping the acceleration fixed. Roughly how does the stopping distance v0^2/(2|a|) change?\"\n  conceptId=\"acceleration\"\n  explanation=\"Stopping distance is proportional to v0 squared, so doubling v0 multiplies the stopping distance by four. This is the v^2 law that drives speed limits and following-distance rules.\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"It roughly doubles.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It roughly quadruples.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It stays about the same.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 1.1: Reading Signs on a Braking Shuttle\n\n**Problem:** A campus shuttle moving to the right at $12\\ \\text{m/s}$ brakes uniformly and stops in $4.0\\ \\text{s}$. Taking right as positive, find the acceleration and the stopping distance.\n\n**Step 1: Set up kinematics.** The motion is rectilinear with constant acceleration. Known: $v_0 = +12\\ \\text{m/s}$, final $v = 0$, $t = 4.0\\ \\text{s}$.\n\n**Step 2: Solve for acceleration** using $v = v_0 + at$:\n\n$$\na = \\frac{v - v_0}{t} = \\frac{0 - 12}{4.0} = \\boxed{-3.0\\ \\text{m/s}^2}.\n$$\n\nThe acceleration is negative: it opposes the positive velocity, which is exactly Case 2 in the sign table (moving right, slowing down).\n\n**Step 3: Stopping distance** from $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\,\\Delta x$ with $v = 0$:\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\frac{-v_0^2}{2a} = \\frac{-(12)^2}{2(-3.0)} = \\frac{-144}{-6.0} = \\boxed{24\\ \\text{m}}.\n$$\n\nThe negative signs cancel, giving a positive distance, as physical sense demands.\n\n---\n\n### Example 1.2: Same Distance, Twice the Speed\n\n**Problem:** Suppose the same shuttle brakes with the same deceleration magnitude $3.0\\ \\text{m/s}^2$, but now starts at $24\\ \\text{m/s}$ (double the speed). How does the stopping distance change?\n\n**Step 1: Apply the time-free equation** again with $v = 0$:\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\frac{-v_0^2}{2a} = \\frac{-(24)^2}{2(-3.0)} = \\frac{-576}{-6.0} = \\boxed{96\\ \\text{m}}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Interpret.** Doubling the speed from 12 to 24 m/s did not double the stopping distance from 24 to 48 m; it quadrupled it to 96 m. Because $\\Delta x \\propto v_0^2$, stopping distance scales with the *square* of speed. This single fact drives highway speed limits, following-distance rules, and automatic-emergency-braking design budgets.\n\n> **Think About It:** The same $v^2$ dependence is why kinetic energy is $\\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$. Energy and stopping distance both punish speed quadratically.\n\n---\n\n### Example 1.3: From Force to Motion (Kinetics)\n\n**Problem:** A $1{,}200\\ \\text{kg}$ car experiences a net rearward braking force of $7{,}200\\ \\text{N}$ while moving forward at $30\\ \\text{m/s}$. Find its acceleration and the distance to stop.\n\n**Step 1: Kinetics, Newton's second law.** With forward as positive, the net force is $-7{,}200\\ \\text{N}$:\n\n$$\n\\sum F = m a \\;\\Rightarrow\\; a = \\frac{\\sum F}{m} = \\frac{-7{,}200}{1{,}200} = \\boxed{-6.0\\ \\text{m/s}^2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Kinematics, stopping distance** with $v = 0$, $v_0 = 30\\ \\text{m/s}$:\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\frac{-v_0^2}{2a} = \\frac{-(30)^2}{2(-6.0)} = \\frac{-900}{-12.0} = \\boxed{75\\ \\text{m}}.\n$$\n\nThis example shows the full dynamics loop: kinetics ($F \\to a$) hands its result to kinematics ($a \\to \\Delta x$). That hand-off, force in one column and motion in the other linked by acceleration, is the workflow you will repeat for the rest of the course.\n\nStep through that loop yourself before re-reading the solution. Commit to a prediction at each stage, then reveal how the force becomes an acceleration and the acceleration becomes a stopping distance.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Walking the kinetics-to-kinematics loop (Example 1.3)\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Forward is positive and the braking force is 7,200 N rearward. What is the SIGN of the net force on the car?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Positive, because the car is still moving forward\",\"Negative, because the force opposes the forward motion\",\"Zero, because the car has not stopped yet\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The net force is -7,200 N. Sign comes from the force direction, not the velocity: the brake force points backward, opposite the chosen positive (forward) direction.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Use Newton second law a = F/m with m = 1,200 kg. Which acceleration is correct?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"a = -6.0 m/s^2\",\"a = +6.0 m/s^2\",\"a = -0.167 m/s^2\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"a = (-7,200)/(1,200) = -6.0 m/s^2. Kinetics has now produced the acceleration; it is negative because it opposes the forward velocity.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Now hand a to kinematics. With v0 = 30 m/s and final v = 0, which equation gives the stopping distance without needing time?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"x = x0 + v0 t + 1/2 a t^2\",\"v^2 = v0^2 + 2 a (x - x0)\",\"v = v0 + a t\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The time-free relation v^2 = v0^2 + 2 a (delta x) is the right tool because time is unknown and not asked for.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Solving: delta x = -v0^2/(2a) = -(30)^2 / (2*(-6.0)) = -900 / -12.0 = 75 m. The negatives cancel, giving a positive distance, and the kinetics-to-kinematics loop is complete.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n## Why Engineers Care\n\nDynamics is not abstract bookkeeping. These quantities drive design decisions:\n\n- braking distance depends on how velocity and acceleration evolve over time\n- passenger comfort depends on how abruptly acceleration changes (the rate of change of acceleration, called *jerk*)\n- actuator sizing depends on how quickly a mechanism must change speed\n- safety margins depend on whether a structure experiences smooth loading or sudden impact\n\nWhen we model motion well, we can move from intuition to prediction.\n\n<dynamic-scenario\n  topic=\"Velocity and Acceleration in Vehicle Motion\"\n  context=\"A campus shuttle leaves Bryant-Denny Stadium after a game. It accelerates away from the curb, brakes for pedestrians, then merges back into traffic. Explain how velocity and acceleration help the engineer reason about safety and rider comfort.\"\n  course=\"dynamics\"\n></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## A Useful Mental Model\n\nOne of the cleanest ways to begin dynamics is to separate three questions: where is the body (position `x(t)`), how fast is its position changing (velocity `v = dx/dt`), and how fast is that velocity changing (acceleration `a = dv/dt`). Later in the course, these derivatives become the bridge between geometry, forces, energy, and momentum.\n\n## Modeling Assumptions at the Start of Dynamics\n\nThe first dynamics models in a course are deliberately simple. Most of them assume:\n\n- the body can be treated as a particle unless rotation is the main issue,\n- mass remains constant,\n- the reference frame is approximately inertial,\n- and effects such as drag, structural flexibility, and control delay are small enough to ignore.\n\nThose assumptions are not weaknesses. They are what make first-order prediction possible. But they should always be visible in the engineer's mind, because every omitted effect can become important when speeds rise, tolerances tighten, or comfort and control matter. The Curiosity landing, for instance, *could not* ignore drag or changing mass: the atmosphere did most of the braking, and the spacecraft shed mass as it jettisoned its heat shield and parachute.\n\n## Engineering Trade-Off: Simplicity Versus Fidelity\n\nOne of the recurring choices in dynamics is how much detail to include. A simpler model is faster to build, easier to debug, and often sufficient for early design screening. A richer model may capture aerodynamic drag, actuator lag, structural compliance, ride comfort, or sensor uncertainty, but every added effect raises modeling cost and data requirements. Strong engineering judgment means choosing the **simplest model that is still trustworthy for the decision being made**.\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | What dynamics predicts | Quantity that governs the design |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Automotive braking systems | Stopping distance from a given speed | $\\Delta x = v_0^2 / (2|a|)$ |\n| Spacecraft entry, descent, landing | Velocity profile through the atmosphere | $v(t) = v_0 + \\int a\\,dt$ |\n| Industrial robot arms | Time to reach a target pose | $x = x_0 + v_0 t + \\tfrac12 a t^2$ |\n| Elevator ride comfort | Acceptable acceleration and jerk levels | $a = dv/dt$, jerk $= da/dt$ |\n| Conveyor and packaging lines | Start/stop without product slip | $\\sum F = ma$ at the contact |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** The same three quantities (position, velocity, acceleration) describe a robot gripper moving 0.2 m and a rover decelerating across 5,900 m/s. Only the numbers change; the differentiation-and-integration logic is identical.\n\n---\n\n## Common Beginner Mistakes\n\n- Confusing **velocity** with **acceleration**\n- Assuming an object that is \"slowing down\" must have negative velocity (it only has acceleration opposite to its velocity)\n- Forgetting that acceleration can be nonzero even when speed is constant but direction is changing\n- Using constant-acceleration equations when the acceleration is actually varying with time, position, or velocity\n\nWhen you feel stuck in dynamics, it is often because one of those four confusions is hiding inside the setup.\n\n## How These Ideas Connect\n\nThe four core ideas of this section are not a list; they form a small network. Select a node to see how kinematics, kinetics, velocity, and acceleration relate, with acceleration sitting at the center as the bridge between describing motion and explaining its cause.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"kinematics\",\"dynamics\",\"velocity\",\"acceleration\"]' title=\"Introduction to Dynamics\"></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A car is moving to the right, but its speed is decreasing. If right is defined as positive, which statement must be true?\"\n  conceptId=\"acceleration\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Its velocity and acceleration are both positive.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Its velocity is positive and its acceleration is negative.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Its velocity is negative and its acceleration is positive.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Its velocity is zero because it is slowing down.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Exactly. The car is still moving right, so velocity is positive. Because its speed is decreasing, the acceleration must point opposite the motion, which makes it negative in this sign convention.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Why is the distinction between kinematics and kinetics so important in engineering dynamics?\"\n  conceptId=\"kinematics\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because kinematics and kinetics are just two words for the same equations.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because kinematics describes the motion, while kinetics explains the forces and moments causing it.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because kinetics is only used for chemistry problems, not mechanics.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Right. Kinematics tells us what the motion looks like; kinetics tells us why it is happening. Strong problem solving in dynamics depends on knowing when you are describing motion and when you are explaining its cause.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation | When it applies |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Velocity (definition) | $v = \\dfrac{dx}{dt}$ | Always |\n| Acceleration (definition) | $a = \\dfrac{dv}{dt} = \\dfrac{d^2x}{dt^2}$ | Always |\n| Position (constant $a$) | $x = x_0 + v_0 t + \\tfrac{1}{2} a t^2$ | Constant acceleration only |\n| Time-free relation | $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\,(x - x_0)$ | Constant acceleration only |\n| Newton's second law | $\\sum \\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$ | Particle, constant mass, inertial frame |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Dynamics predicts how motion changes; kinematics describes it and kinetics explains its cause.\n- Acceleration is the bridge: forces produce it, and integrating it recovers velocity and position.\n- A body speeds up when $v$ and $a$ share a sign, and slows down when they oppose.\n- Stopping distance scales with the *square* of speed, a direct consequence of $v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a\\,\\Delta x$.\n- The constant-acceleration equations are valid only when $a$ is truly constant; otherwise return to the integrals.\n\n---\n\n## Looking Ahead\n\nIn the next sections, we will formalize rectilinear motion and start building predictive equations for particles moving along a line. The goal is not just to manipulate symbols. It is to build a disciplined habit: describe motion clearly first, then connect that motion to the forces that produce it.\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"rectilinear_concept_1","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"A car is moving to the right and slowing down. What is the sign of its acceleration (if right is positive)?","options":["Positive (same as velocity)","Negative (opposite to velocity)","Zero (since it's slowing)","Cannot be determined"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"When an object slows down, acceleration opposes velocity. If velocity is positive (right), acceleration is negative (left).","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["acceleration","velocity"],"misconception_id":"sign_confusion"},{"id":"rectilinear_concept_2","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"Which kinematic equation should you use if you know v₀, a, and s, and want to find v (without needing t)?","options":["v = v₀ + at","s = v₀t + ½at²","v² = v₀² + 2a(s - s₀)","s = ½(v₀ + v)t"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The equation v² = v₀² + 2a(s - s₀) relates velocity and position without time.","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["kinematics"],"misconception_id":"wrong_equation"},{"id":"rectilinear_concept_3","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"Can you use s = v₀t + ½at² when acceleration is not constant?","options":["Yes, always","No, only for constant acceleration","Only if a is decreasing","Only if v₀ = 0"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The constant acceleration kinematic equations are derived assuming a = constant. For variable a, you must integrate.","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["kinematics","acceleration"],"misconception_id":"variable_acceleration"},{"id":"rectilinear_concept_4","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"If you double the initial velocity of a braking car (same deceleration), the stopping distance becomes:","options":["Double","Quadruple (4×)","Half","Same"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"From v² = v₀² + 2as, with v = 0: s = v₀²/(2a). Doubling v₀ quadruples s.","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["kinematics","velocity"],"misconception_id":"wrong_equation"},{"id":"rectilinear_numeric_1","type":"numeric","solver_id":"solve_kinematics_vf","stem":"A particle starts from rest and accelerates at 3 m/s² for 8 seconds. Find its final velocity.","params":{"v0":0,"a":3,"t":8},"hints":["Use v = v₀ + at","v₀ = 0 since it starts from rest"],"final_answer":{"value":24,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["kinematics","velocity","acceleration"]},{"id":"rectilinear_numeric_2","type":"numeric","solver_id":"solve_kinematics_distance","stem":"A car traveling at 30 m/s brakes with a = -6 m/s². How far does it travel before stopping?","params":{"v0":30,"v":0,"a":-6},"hints":["Use v² = v₀² + 2as and solve for s","The car stops when v = 0"],"final_answer":{"value":75,"unit":"m","tolerance":0.5},"difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["kinematics","velocity","acceleration"]},{"id":"rectilinear_numeric_3","type":"numeric","solver_id":"solve_kinematics_time","stem":"A ball is dropped from 80 m. How long does it take to reach the ground? (g = 10 m/s²)","params":{"s0":0,"s":80,"v0":0,"a":10},"hints":["Use s = v₀t + ½at² with v₀ = 0","Solve for t"],"final_answer":{"value":4,"unit":"s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["kinematics","acceleration"]},{"id":"rectilinear_numeric_4","type":"numeric","solver_id":"solve_kinematics_accel","stem":"A rocket accelerates from 0 to 100 m/s over a distance of 500 m. What is its acceleration?","params":{"v0":0,"v":100,"s":500},"hints":["Use v² = v₀² + 2as","Solve for a"],"final_answer":{"value":10,"unit":"m/s²","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["kinematics","acceleration","velocity"]},{"id":"rectilinear_step_1","type":"step_based","solver_id":"solve_kinematics_steps","stem":"A train traveling at 20 m/s begins to decelerate at 2 m/s². Find the distance traveled in 6 seconds and the final velocity.","params":{"v0":20,"a":-2,"t":6},"steps":[{"step_id":1,"description":"Calculate final velocity using v = v₀ + at","expected_value":8,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1},{"step_id":2,"description":"Calculate distance using s = v₀t + ½at²","expected_value":84,"unit":"m","tolerance":0.5}],"final_answer":{"value":84,"unit":"m","tolerance":0.5},"difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["kinematics","velocity","acceleration"]},{"id":"rectilinear_step_2","type":"step_based","solver_id":"solve_two_phase_motion","stem":"A car accelerates from rest at 4 m/s² for 5 s, then maintains constant velocity for 10 s. Find total distance.","params":{"a1":4,"t1":5,"t2":10},"steps":[{"step_id":1,"description":"Find velocity at end of acceleration phase: v = at₁","expected_value":20,"unit":"m/s","tolerance":0.1},{"step_id":2,"description":"Find distance during acceleration: s₁ = ½at₁²","expected_value":50,"unit":"m","tolerance":0.5},{"step_id":3,"description":"Find distance during constant velocity: s₂ = v·t₂","expected_value":200,"unit":"m","tolerance":0.5},{"step_id":4,"description":"Find total distance: s = s₁ + s₂","expected_value":250,"unit":"m","tolerance":0.5}],"final_answer":{"value":250,"unit":"m","tolerance":0.5},"difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["kinematics","velocity","acceleration"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"sign_confusion","pattern":"sign_error","description":"Confusing acceleration sign when object slows down","trigger":"answer has wrong sign but correct magnitude","feedback":"Remember: acceleration is negative when it opposes the direction of velocity. If moving right (+) and slowing, a is negative.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"unit_mismatch","pattern":"unit_error","description":"Missing or incorrect units in answer","trigger":"numeric correct but unit wrong/missing","feedback":"Check your units! Velocity should be in m/s, acceleration in m/s², distance in m.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"wrong_equation","pattern":"equation_selection","description":"Using wrong kinematic equation for the given knowns","trigger":"answer significantly wrong, not a calculation error","feedback":"Make sure to choose the kinematic equation that contains your known quantities. List what you know (v₀, v, a, t, s) and pick accordingly.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"initial_conditions","pattern":"initial_value_error","description":"Forgetting initial velocity or position","trigger":"answer missing v₀ or s₀ term","feedback":"Don't forget initial conditions! 'From rest' means v₀ = 0. 'Initially at origin' means s₀ = 0.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"variable_acceleration","pattern":"method_error","description":"Using constant-a equations when acceleration varies","trigger":"problem states a = f(t) or a = f(v) but student used constant-a formula","feedback":"These equations only work for constant acceleration. For variable a, you must integrate: v = ∫a dt.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"f08d1108ce2aedf0f6d4fb319557d1742a5bf193b87d98155e713b761a3593b5","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}